  "platform": "Neo Geo MVS / AES",
  "summary": "Nazca’s hand-drawn run-and-gun adventure sends Marco and Tarma through six missions full of soldiers, prisoners and expressive machinery.",
  "body": [
    "Metal Slug is Nazca’s side-scrolling run-and-gun game, first released for the Neo Geo arcade system in Japan on 19 April 1996. Marco Rossi and Tarma Roving cross six missions against General Morden’s rebel army, firing at soldiers and much larger machines. One or two players can advance together, finding new guns, throwing grenades and rescuing prisoners along the route. The small tank that gives the game its name can be boarded along the way, adding another layer to a campaign already crowded with moving characters and destructible scenery.",
    "Entering the Metal Slug changes both attack and survival. Its weapons offer greater firepower, while the vehicle absorbs damage that would cost the soldier a life on foot. The player can move between these states as the stage demands, using the tank until it is time to leave it behind. Freed prisoners reward a rescue with supplies, and keeping the rescued total through the end of a mission earns extra points. Losing a life resets that tally, tying careful play to the people saved along the way.",
    "Much of the game’s personality appears in the moments around the shooting. Soldiers cook, talk or relax before noticing the player; machines recoil and collapse through elaborate animation. The scenery can break apart to reveal items, encouraging shots into the surroundings as well as directly at enemies. Several members of Nazca had worked on Irem action games, bringing that experience into a more immediately approachable set of controls. The expressive drawings make the battlefield readable through reactions, turning small enemies and the squat tank into memorable performers."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Marco, Tarma and the Slug",
    "Getting into the vehicle",
    "A battlefield full of reactions"
  ],
